Strict Host Key Checking
1. The StrictHostKeyChecking parameter is set in the user-specific ~/.ssh/config file, or in the system-wide /etc/ssh/ssh_config file, or by specifying 
the -o StrictHostKeyChecking= option of the ssh command.
2. If the StrictHostKeyChecking parameter is set to yes, then the ssh command always aborts the SSH connection if the public keys do not match.
3. If the StrictHostKeyChecking parameter is set to no, then the ssh command enables the connection and adds the key of the target host to 
the ~/.ssh/known_hosts file.
4. If the target host SSH key changed since the last time that you connected to it, then the ssh command asks for confirmation to log in and accept the new key.
5. If you accept the new key, then a copy of the public key is saved in the ~/.ssh/known_hosts file to automatically confirm the server's identity on subsequent connections.
